in my office with questions about earlier exams.

15
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:31,800
Everyone is allowed to do the same thing

16
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:33,160
if you're so inclined.

17
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:35,460
You don't have to physically come to the office, though.

18
00:00:35,460 --> 00:00:39,160
You can also call me, Skype, many possibilities, email,

19
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:40,440
of course.

20
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:41,760
All right.

21
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:45,360
So in the last class, we were doing the famous median voter

22
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:45,860
theorem.

23
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:51,880
Median voter model, which leads to median voter theorem.

24
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:56,760
And we start with this distribution of list points.

25
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:58,720
We usually draw it as normal, but it does not

26
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:01,520
have to be normal.

27
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:05,280
And we realize the first big piece of advice

28
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:09,720
is to move to the center, to moderate.

29
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:13,040
You want to moderate in order to get extra votes.

30
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:15,840
And thus, the first equilibrium condition

31
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:19,160
we see is that the Democratic platform must

32
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:21,120
equal the Republican platform in equilibrium,

33
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:23,760
or whatever the two sides might be.

34
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:29,800
And this is called convergence, where

35
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:31,360
they wind up being the same.

36
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:33,280
As I mentioned, especially today, many people

37
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:34,880
just consider this ridiculous.

38
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:37,800
I say it's because people lack international and historical

39
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:41,320
perspective to realize how, despite the great anger

40
00:01:41,320 --> 00:01:43,280
that the two sides have for one another,

41
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:45,600
they are actually extremely similar in the broad scheme

42
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:46,120
of things.

43
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:47,540
When you think about this, suppose

44
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:49,600
that during the wars of religion in Europe,

45
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:52,400
you said Catholic and Protestant are basically the same.

46
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:54,720
Think about how many people would have torgued you limb

47
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:56,160
from limb for that.

48
00:01:56,160 --> 00:02:00,280
But, well, aren't they basically the same?

49
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:02,360
Reverend Lovejoy of the Simpsons says so.

50
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:02,860
Not so.

51
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:03,360
He doesn't.

52
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:05,700
I mean, consider any of the other major religions now.

53
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:08,040
They're all basically the same.

54
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:09,880
So anyway, that's what I would say there.

55
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:12,200
But I agree.

56
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:14,160
Then there's a question, could they ever

57
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:17,200
both converge to something that is below the median?

58
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:18,840
This is a leading question.

59
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:20,240
Is there any way that could happen?

60
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:21,720
And the answer is no.

61
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:24,600
If they both converge to, say, the 40th percentile,

62
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:25,980
then one party could win for sure

63
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:28,680
by going to the 41st percentile and getting

64
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:32,280
59 plus percent of the vote.

65
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:34,400
So what we know is that they must be converged.

66
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:36,880
So it's not possible for them to actually

67
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:38,560
both be above the median.

68
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:42,440
So the one they converge to has to be greater than or less

69
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:43,680
than or equal to the median.

70
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:45,560
By the same logic, though, they couldn't both

71
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:46,640
be in the 80th percentile.

72
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:48,840
Then one could just move down to 79.

73
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:53,480
And then they would win with 79% plus of the vote.

74
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:55,000
If you're the puzzle.

75
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:56,680
Yeah, the second equation.

76
00:02:56,680 --> 00:02:59,040
Yeah, so the second equation, this

77
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:01,520
is the one where, let's see, so you've

78
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:04,640
got to both be less than or equal to the median.

79
00:03:04,640 --> 00:03:08,280
And you also have to be greater than or equal to the median.

80
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:11,200
And how can both those equations be true?

81
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:13,080
If you were exactly equal to the median.

82
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:16,680
So yes, so if you're both in the 50th percentile, what happens

83
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:18,840
if either one of you unilaterally

84
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:21,200
moves a little to the left, a little to the right,

85
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:24,080
you then lose for sure.

86
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:27,360
So therefore, we have the famous median voter

87
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:31,560
theorem, which is PD equals PR equals

88
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:35,280
PMED, which is the bliss point of the median

89
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:37,240
of the distribution.

90
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:39,160
Now suppose you happen to be the median.

91
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:41,000
Does this mean that if you change your mind,

92
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,760
the world changes?

93
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:45,040
You are human.

94
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:47,360
You are exactly the median person

95
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:48,880
in the world and the country.

96
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:50,600
And then if you wake up and you say, hey,

97
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:54,160
I've changed my mind about eating meat.

98
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:55,120
I'm a vegan now.

99
00:03:55,120 --> 00:03:57,760
Since I'm the median voter, now the country will go vegan.

100
00:03:57,760 --> 00:03:59,560
Is that how it works?

101
00:03:59,560 --> 00:04:02,000
No, because when the median voter person changes his mind,

102
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
he ceases to be the median.

103
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,560
Man, so I was all powerful as long as I was normal.

104
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:11,080
But as soon as I became abnormal,

105
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:13,320
then I ceased to be all powerful.

106
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:16,160
So it's not that great of a kind of power,

107
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:18,040
because the kind of power you only

108
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:23,080
have as long as you remain exactly median.

109
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:23,720
Yes?

110
00:04:23,720 --> 00:04:26,320
Does it change with electoral rules?

111
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:27,920
Sure, well, if you had a rule saying

112
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:30,000
that foreigners could vote, for example,

113
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:31,240
that would change the median.

114
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:31,920
And that, yes.

115
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:36,600
I meant, like, for example, if you have two rounds.

116
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:37,960
Oh, if you have two?

117
00:04:37,960 --> 00:04:40,240
Like two successive, like, first of all,

118
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:41,640
you have one round, two laps.

119
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:42,280
Oh, two rounds.

120
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:42,960
Two rounds, yes.

121
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:43,460
Oh, yeah.

122
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:45,920
So that's a great question.

123
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:47,920
One application of the median voter model

124
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:49,460
that many people like is to say, well,

125
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:53,240
it explains why during primaries everyone acts extreme.

126
00:04:53,240 --> 00:04:55,280
And then once they get to the general election,

127
00:04:55,280 --> 00:04:58,120
then everyone stops acting extreme.

128
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:00,200
And the story there is when you're in the primary,

129
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:02,680
you're trying to get the affection of the median

130
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:04,680
Democrat or the median Republican.

131
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:07,760
Or in fact, it's even more than that.

132
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:10,160
The median of the people who are so committed,

133
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:11,640
they vote in primaries.

134
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:15,520
So in 2016, I think you have usually only about 10% or 15%

135
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:18,000
of eligible people voting in primaries.

136
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:19,960
So the people that will are probably

137
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:23,600
going to be very liberal or very conservative relative to even

138
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:26,680
the other people who identify with the party.

139
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:28,840
So yes, there's a common story about why

140
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:31,440
you go and act more liberal or conservative,

141
00:05:31,440 --> 00:05:33,800
or act very liberal or conservative in the primaries.

142
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:35,760
And then once it comes to the general election,

143
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:38,880
then you say, oh, yeah, I didn't mean any of that stuff.

144
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:40,400
There is a puzzle about why people

145
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:42,400
would go and elect someone that actually

146
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:44,440
appears to be unelectable.

147
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:47,280
Or why it is that people couldn't just

148
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:50,360
use every word you said in the primary against you.

149
00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:53,200
Interesting question.

150
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:55,480
This is the famous median voter theorem,

151
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:58,520
and an extremely useful model, which

152
00:05:58,520 --> 00:06:00,760
explains so much about the world.

153
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:05,040
Why is it that we didn't have gay marriage in 1900?

154
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:06,720
Why?

155
00:06:06,720 --> 00:06:07,560
You've got no idea.

156
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:10,120
Why did we not have gay marriage in 1900?

157
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:11,320
Median voter disapproved it.

158
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:13,040
Median voter really did not want it.

159
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:14,580
Median voter wouldn't have understood

160
00:06:14,580 --> 00:06:15,880
what you're talking about.

161
00:06:15,880 --> 00:06:17,760
Well, of course marriage should be gay.

162
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:20,640
We should all be gay all the time.

163
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:22,200
All right.

164
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:26,480
And whereas now, why is that?

165
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:27,800
It's not just the US.

166
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:29,960
It's a huge list of countries that have done it.

167
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:34,080
And the median voter model says, now, what's happened?

168
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:36,120
What's happened now?

169
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:36,720
Yeah, morally.

170
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:40,840
The median voter median of a median person changed his mind.

171
00:06:40,840 --> 00:06:43,680
Whether it's decay or progress, the median voter model

172
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:45,680
doesn't care about those labels.

173
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:47,680
Just cares about what wins.

174
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:50,760
So this explains the reason why the redistribution too, right?

175
00:06:50,760 --> 00:06:51,280
Yeah, sure.

176
00:06:51,280 --> 00:06:53,720
So I mean, you know that the middle is the richer people.

177
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:54,440
Yeah, sure, sure.

178
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:58,880
And actually, if you take my graduate public choice class,

179
00:06:58,880 --> 00:07:01,000
we talk about that.

180
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,640
So the simple version where it's just poor people

181
00:07:03,640 --> 00:07:05,640
vote for redistribution, rich people vote against

182
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:06,160
is so wrong.

183
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:07,880
It's ridiculous.

184
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:11,080
But the general point of why do we have redistribution?

185
00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:13,640
Because the median person wants redistribution.

186
00:07:13,640 --> 00:07:16,880
Oftentimes, it turns out the median person is quite well off

187
00:07:16,880 --> 00:07:17,800
and is a net payer.

188
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:19,600
But still, they vote for it.

189
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:22,040
So you could potentially say that just because the median

190
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:23,920
voter wants something doesn't necessarily

191
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:25,640
mean it's a good idea.

192
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:28,480
Well, you're preaching to the choir there, yes.

193
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:30,480
In fact, I think I prefer the other rule.

194
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:33,240
If they want it, it's probably a bad idea.

195
00:07:33,240 --> 00:07:37,000
I mean, it's more predictive if you're comparing different times

196
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:37,800
within a country.

197
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:41,480
If we were to compare America now to Sweden now,

198
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:43,840
then this loses a lot of its predictive power.

199
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:46,280
But if we were to compare America in 1900,

200
00:07:46,280 --> 00:07:48,640
when a lot of people were disenfranchised from voting,

201
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:50,920
to America now, that's a lot more predictive.

202
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:53,840
Wouldn't actually have been that many disenfranchised in the US.

203
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:55,960
And so, yeah, you have to go back further.

204
00:07:55,960 --> 00:07:58,080
Go back further, like 1820 or something.

205
00:07:58,080 --> 00:07:58,760
Yeah.

206
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:01,280
So in my public choice class, we get a little bit of this.

207
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:03,800
By the way, this lecture is a commercial for that class.

208
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:06,440
So if you like this one, you can do it for 14 weeks.

209
00:08:06,440 --> 00:08:08,320
But wait, there's more.

210
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:09,840
That's right.

211
00:08:09,840 --> 00:08:10,760
All right.

212
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:13,040
So, and you have so many other cases

213
00:08:13,040 --> 00:08:14,400
that you can go to apply this to.

214
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:17,240
Why does the US have a death penalty, but let's see,

215
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:19,160
yeah, Canada does not, right?

216
00:08:19,160 --> 00:08:23,760
Well, the median voter in America

217
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:24,920
likes the death penalty.

218
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:27,640
Median person in Canada does not like it.

219
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:28,120
All right.

220
00:08:28,120 --> 00:08:30,360
Now, of course, if you know a lot of public opinion,

221
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:32,640
you will start finding deviations of this.

222
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:35,840
For example, according to the public opinion information

223
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:39,520
I've seen, the median in Britain does favor the death penalty.

224
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:41,080
But they don't have it.

225
00:08:41,080 --> 00:08:44,320
And the usual story is that the Oxbridge people who

226
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:47,080
run the country consider it uncouth to have a death

227
00:08:47,080 --> 00:08:48,040
penalty.

228
00:08:48,040 --> 00:08:51,200
And they are not populous enough to have a referendum on that

229
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:52,000
yet.

230
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,320
And if there were, then maybe that would happen.

231
00:08:54,320 --> 00:08:57,400
Certainly with Brexit, among the actual elite

232
00:08:57,400 --> 00:09:00,120
probably would have had very low support.

233
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:01,960
And then it got turned over to a referendum.

234
00:09:01,960 --> 00:09:04,440
So interesting case.

235
00:09:04,440 --> 00:09:05,480
All right.

236
00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:08,320
OK, and so you can apply this to so many cases.

237
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:11,600
It's really endless applications.

238
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:16,240
Doesn't mean it's true, but it's at minimum a very useful

239
00:09:16,240 --> 00:09:16,960
starting point.

240
00:09:16,960 --> 00:09:19,340
Like, the United States is the one developed country that

241
00:09:19,340 --> 00:09:21,080
doesn't have universal health care.

242
00:09:21,080 --> 00:09:23,880
Well, what's the likely explanation for that?

243
00:09:23,880 --> 00:09:26,040
The United States is the one developed country

244
00:09:26,040 --> 00:09:32,240
that has a median voter who does not want universal health care.

245
00:09:32,240 --> 00:09:32,740
Yes?

246
00:09:32,740 --> 00:09:34,280
How does this work when you apply it

247
00:09:34,280 --> 00:09:37,080
to parliaments that have proportional representation?

248
00:09:37,080 --> 00:09:39,000
Ah, so that's a great question.

249
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,120
So as I think I was mentioning last class,

250
00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:44,000
it is normal, though not universal,

251
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,400
for parliaments to split down into two voting blocks,

252
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:48,160
two sides.

253
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:50,400
And then as long as that's going on,

254
00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:52,440
then this model still works OK.

255
00:09:52,440 --> 00:09:55,040
If there's genuinely three groups,

256
00:09:55,040 --> 00:09:57,240
then the model doesn't work very well,

257
00:09:57,240 --> 00:09:58,360
or at least theoretically.

258
00:09:58,360 --> 00:10:00,400
And actually, just like with perfect competition,

259
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:02,440
the median voter model seems to work a lot better

260
00:10:02,440 --> 00:10:06,120
than it should, because even in cases where the assumptions are

261
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:07,640
clearly wrong, the model still seems

262
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:10,000
to be quite predictive in both cases.

263
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,120
So there's no.

264
00:10:12,120 --> 00:10:13,000
All right.

265
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:13,960
Now, let's see.

266
00:10:13,960 --> 00:10:17,720
I see there's some weird error in the lettering,

267
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:19,520
but you can still follow.

268
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:20,480
All right.

269
00:10:20,480 --> 00:10:22,400
Now, of course, this began with the assumption

270
00:10:22,400 --> 00:10:26,520
that voters actually know what policies the parties favor.

271
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:28,400
And when people looked into the question,

272
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:32,120
how much do voters actually know about this stuff?

273
00:10:32,120 --> 00:10:36,200
Well, remember the first theory?

274
00:10:36,200 --> 00:10:38,200
Suggest that people are going to balance out

275
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:40,360
the marginal benefits and the marginal costs

276
00:10:40,360 --> 00:10:42,320
of acquiring additional information about what's

277
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:43,840
going on in politics.

278
00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:45,360
Remember that?

279
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:50,000
But then, not to spoil the whole story,

280
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,240
but just as a rough approximation,

281
00:10:52,240 --> 00:10:53,800
what do you think the probability is

282
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:55,720
that one individual American's vote determines

283
00:10:55,720 --> 00:10:59,280
the outcome of a presidential election?

284
00:10:59,280 --> 00:11:00,920
Yeah, approximately zero.

285
00:11:00,920 --> 00:11:02,720
There's a lot of dispute about whether it's

286
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:06,280
like one in 10 million or one in a Google.

287
00:11:06,280 --> 00:11:07,680
All right.

288
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:11,280
So there's active debate on that,

289
00:11:11,280 --> 00:11:15,560
but everyone's basically saying that it's approximately zero.

290
00:11:15,560 --> 00:11:16,640
All right.

291
00:11:16,640 --> 00:11:21,840
So what this means is that the expected amount

292
00:11:21,840 --> 00:11:24,160
of marginal benefit of getting more information

293
00:11:24,160 --> 00:11:27,680
is also almost certainly zero by extension,

294
00:11:27,680 --> 00:11:30,200
because what good does it do to learn something

295
00:11:30,200 --> 00:11:34,320
if you can't change anything on the basis of the information?

296
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:36,840
Suppose you knew everything about politics.

297
00:11:36,840 --> 00:11:38,880
Would that mean that people would listen to you?

298
00:11:41,600 --> 00:11:43,720
I mean, you might sit around trying to leverage it

299
00:11:43,720 --> 00:11:45,840
into more attention, like I'm going

300
00:11:45,840 --> 00:11:48,480
to predict everything that will happen for the next three

301
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:51,880
years, and if I get it all right,

302
00:11:51,880 --> 00:11:53,960
then will you listen to me?

303
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:55,600
And probably people say, well, he

304
00:11:55,600 --> 00:11:58,000
may be very good at predicting the things that

305
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,240
can be described, but he's terrible at everything else.

306
00:12:01,240 --> 00:12:03,320
So it might be something like that.

307
00:12:03,320 --> 00:12:06,240
So anyway, so we got positive marginal cost

308
00:12:06,240 --> 00:12:08,880
of acquiring political information.

309
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:11,280
What folks saying explains why there's

310
00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:13,440
positive marginal cost?

311
00:12:13,440 --> 00:12:16,120
Time is money.

312
00:12:16,120 --> 00:12:17,320
Time is money, son.

313
00:12:17,320 --> 00:12:19,160
Time is money.

314
00:12:19,160 --> 00:12:21,000
So if you got positive marginal cost

315
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,920
of getting political information and approximately zero

316
00:12:23,920 --> 00:12:26,240
benefits, this would seem to predict

317
00:12:26,240 --> 00:12:28,360
that people would optimally acquire

318
00:12:28,360 --> 00:12:30,880
how much political information?

319
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:31,920
None.

320
00:12:31,920 --> 00:12:33,600
People would know nothing.

321
00:12:33,600 --> 00:12:35,800
And this is the origin of the famous concept

322
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:39,080
of rational ignorance.

323
00:12:39,080 --> 00:12:42,560
So if knowledge gives you no practical benefit,

324
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:44,480
and then again, why is it not?

325
00:12:44,480 --> 00:12:46,920
Isn't it good to know what good policies are?

326
00:12:46,920 --> 00:12:51,040
Only if people listen to you and they don't.

327
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:52,240
You may have noticed this.

328
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:53,780
Have you ever figured everything out

329
00:12:53,780 --> 00:12:56,880
and then people didn't listen to you?

330
00:12:56,880 --> 00:12:59,560
Ever have everything worked out in great detail?

331
00:12:59,560 --> 00:13:01,440
And you go, I have figured it out.

332
00:13:01,440 --> 00:13:03,440
And the world doesn't listen to you.

333
00:13:03,440 --> 00:13:09,960
Even on something where the answer is just so clear,

334
00:13:09,960 --> 00:13:12,760
so non-ideological, it's like, well,

335
00:13:12,760 --> 00:13:14,680
the amount of money that we spend on foreign aid

336
00:13:14,680 --> 00:13:16,400
is not half the budget.

337
00:13:16,400 --> 00:13:17,640
Oh, you lie.

338
00:13:17,640 --> 00:13:18,140
Liar.

339
00:13:18,140 --> 00:13:20,560
All right, that kind of thing.

340
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:22,840
So anyway, the concept of rational ignorance

341
00:13:22,840 --> 00:13:26,320
is a natural byproduct of this analysis,

342
00:13:26,320 --> 00:13:29,440
saying that the decision not to acquire information

343
00:13:29,440 --> 00:13:31,920
is rational when you're not getting anything out of it

344
00:13:31,920 --> 00:13:33,840
and time is money.

345
00:13:33,840 --> 00:13:36,840
Now, literally, of course, this is not true.

346
00:13:36,840 --> 00:13:40,320
Because almost all Americans know the name of the president.

347
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:45,040
And a very good number also know how many years he serves.

348
00:13:45,040 --> 00:13:48,640
And they also know the name of the country.

349
00:13:48,640 --> 00:13:50,480
We know that.

350
00:13:50,480 --> 00:13:52,720
All right, and then we start running low on things

351
00:13:52,720 --> 00:13:55,560
almost everybody knows.

352
00:13:55,560 --> 00:13:58,320
So knowing the name of the vice president,

353
00:13:58,320 --> 00:14:01,960
that's something we're usually down to 75%.

354
00:14:01,960 --> 00:14:04,280
And then go a bit further, like knowing

355
00:14:04,280 --> 00:14:06,920
that each state has two senators.

356
00:14:06,920 --> 00:14:08,920
Now, this is a rule which has changed how many times

357
00:14:08,920 --> 00:14:10,640
in American history?

358
00:14:10,640 --> 00:14:11,880
Zero.

359
00:14:11,880 --> 00:14:14,600
And is this a really hard to remember number?

360
00:14:14,600 --> 00:14:19,320
Like 230,117?

361
00:14:19,320 --> 00:14:20,880
No, it's the number two.

362
00:14:20,880 --> 00:14:23,720
And are you taught this number often in school?

363
00:14:23,720 --> 00:14:25,040
Yes, I was.

364
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:29,320
And yet, half of Americans don't know the answer to that.

365
00:14:29,320 --> 00:14:31,960
All right, and this has been found in many surveys.

366
00:14:31,960 --> 00:14:33,760
So going back a long time.

367
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:36,440
And by the way, it was never any better, as far as I can tell.

368
00:14:36,440 --> 00:14:38,400
So the early surveys go back to the 30s.

369
00:14:38,400 --> 00:14:40,080
And you say, well, back in the old days,

370
00:14:40,080 --> 00:14:42,560
when public schools really taught students,

371
00:14:42,560 --> 00:14:46,080
well, the good old days must precede the 30s.

372
00:14:46,080 --> 00:14:49,840
Because we have to go back to a time when it is data free.

373
00:14:49,840 --> 00:14:52,320
And then we can believe anything we feel like believing.

374
00:14:52,320 --> 00:14:55,200
But during any measured time, things

375
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:57,720
have never been noticeably better.

376
00:14:57,720 --> 00:15:00,760
Doesn't Wiseman challenge this by saying that?

377
00:15:00,760 --> 00:15:01,440
We'll get to it.

378
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:02,320
Don't worry.

379
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:04,040
We will get to it.

380
00:15:04,040 --> 00:15:05,520
Basic facts first.

381
00:15:08,880 --> 00:15:12,000
And then, let's see.

382
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:13,080
Oh, yeah.

383
00:15:13,080 --> 00:15:14,920
My favorite one is possible.

384
00:15:14,920 --> 00:15:18,000
Well, maybe the senators is close to my favorite one.

385
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:21,280
But just knowing which party controls each house of

386
00:15:21,280 --> 00:15:26,400
Congress, that usually gets up to just a little bit over 50%.

387
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:28,800
And now, what would random guessing get you?

388
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:29,600
50%.

389
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:30,720
50%.

390
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:34,280
So how important is it to know which party controls which

391
00:15:34,280 --> 00:15:35,080
house of the Senate?

392
00:15:35,080 --> 00:15:36,700
How much does that explain about what's

393
00:15:36,700 --> 00:15:40,000
going on in American politics at any given time?

394
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,120
Enormously important.

395
00:15:42,120 --> 00:15:46,680
And yet, most people manage to not know this.

396
00:15:46,680 --> 00:15:47,180
Yeah?

397
00:15:47,180 --> 00:15:48,640
I mean, is it enormously important

398
00:15:48,640 --> 00:15:51,120
if you take the median voter theory seriously?

399
00:15:51,120 --> 00:15:53,520
Ah, good question.

400
00:15:53,520 --> 00:15:54,040
Well, yeah.

401
00:15:54,040 --> 00:15:56,680
So I guess the only thing is the median voter model

402
00:15:56,680 --> 00:15:58,840
presumes that people actually know what's going on.

403
00:15:58,840 --> 00:16:01,760
So they can actually punish or reward people

404
00:16:01,760 --> 00:16:03,160
for doing what they want.

405
00:16:03,160 --> 00:16:07,040
So people really, like, if you combine the median voter

406
00:16:07,040 --> 00:16:09,280
framework with complete cluelessness,

407
00:16:09,280 --> 00:16:12,840
this would seem to mean that the president could

408
00:16:12,840 --> 00:16:15,440
do something bad, and then maybe people get angry at Congress

409
00:16:15,440 --> 00:16:18,400
because they belong to the opposite party.

410
00:16:18,400 --> 00:16:20,560
This might mean that you might want to deliberately

411
00:16:20,560 --> 00:16:25,400
cause a disaster in order to, or not just not divert one, say,

412
00:16:25,400 --> 00:16:28,960
in order to put the other party into a bad light.

413
00:16:28,960 --> 00:16:31,320
I remember back in 2010, my dad, who

414
00:16:31,320 --> 00:16:34,640
was a very strong conservative Republican, just saying, look,

415
00:16:34,640 --> 00:16:37,000
I don't want the economy to brew this year.

416
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:37,720
He told me that.

417
00:16:37,720 --> 00:16:40,880
I do not want the economy to brew this year.

418
00:16:40,880 --> 00:16:43,960
All right, now, figure that out of people that are,

419
00:16:43,960 --> 00:16:46,240
people who are saying that are a small subset of people

420
00:16:46,240 --> 00:16:47,200
who are thinking it.

421
00:16:47,200 --> 00:16:49,640
So maybe that would cause perverse incentives

422
00:16:49,640 --> 00:16:51,920
if you have some divided government.

423
00:16:51,920 --> 00:16:53,800
Or even, it doesn't have to be anything else.

424
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:55,560
It wasn't actually divided in 2010.

425
00:16:55,560 --> 00:16:58,240
Well, in 2000, running up to 2010.

426
00:16:58,240 --> 00:17:01,280
But there were enough people on the other side

427
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:05,200
to stop any kind of bipartisan thinking going forward.

428
00:17:05,200 --> 00:17:06,560
All right.

429
00:17:06,560 --> 00:17:09,480
OK, anyway, so empirically not exactly true,

430
00:17:09,480 --> 00:17:11,880
but it's still not very far from the truth.

431
00:17:11,880 --> 00:17:14,520
And this is the kind of thing where political scientists

432
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:17,720
usually begin by assuming that people are maybe

433
00:17:17,720 --> 00:17:19,840
half as interested in politics as they are.

434
00:17:19,840 --> 00:17:21,760
And they look at the data, and they come away

435
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:24,760
shell-shocked, and like, this has got to be wrong.

436
00:17:24,760 --> 00:17:25,680
It's got to be wrong.

437
00:17:25,680 --> 00:17:27,880
And then after looking at the data long enough,

438
00:17:27,880 --> 00:17:29,920
like, hmm, I guess it's not wrong.

439
00:17:29,920 --> 00:17:31,200
All right, that's weird.

440
00:17:31,200 --> 00:17:33,240
Whereas economists who generally don't know the data all

441
00:17:33,240 --> 00:17:35,120
that well say, yeah, this is what I expected.

442
00:17:35,120 --> 00:17:37,200
Of course, why would people be paying any attention

443
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:37,920
to this stuff?

444
00:17:37,920 --> 00:17:40,200
What's in it for that?

445
00:17:40,200 --> 00:17:42,160
OK, now, the medium-motor model makes

446
00:17:42,160 --> 00:17:43,960
no room for special interests.

447
00:17:43,960 --> 00:17:45,800
Voters just get what they want.

448
00:17:45,800 --> 00:17:50,480
And you can't go and buy a building in Washington

449
00:17:50,480 --> 00:17:53,440
and spend $10 million and then get what you want instead

450
00:17:53,440 --> 00:17:55,480
of what the medium-motor wants.

451
00:17:55,480 --> 00:17:57,240
But there are a lot of people who

452
00:17:57,240 --> 00:17:59,440
have argued that special interests are important.

453
00:17:59,440 --> 00:18:02,040
You might wonder, why are they building buildings down there?

454
00:18:02,040 --> 00:18:04,920
Why do people walk around and see all the weird buildings

455
00:18:04,920 --> 00:18:06,680
that are in DC?

456
00:18:06,680 --> 00:18:10,320
Once a guy came out who was from the American Beef Council.

457
00:18:10,320 --> 00:18:11,960
I don't know what he told me now.

458
00:18:11,960 --> 00:18:14,440
There's actually two different beef lobby organizations,

459
00:18:14,440 --> 00:18:17,000
the American Beef Council and the Beef Council of America.

460
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,600
I've got to be an agent.

461
00:18:19,600 --> 00:18:20,520
They're terrible.

462
00:18:20,520 --> 00:18:22,800
We are totally doing business for beef.

463
00:18:22,800 --> 00:18:24,880
Both groups have their own buildings down there

464
00:18:24,880 --> 00:18:25,600
doing something.

465
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:28,000
I don't know if they have a whole building for themselves.

466
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
But beef is a big product, right?

467
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,840
So it wouldn't surprise me at all if they had a skyscraper.

468
00:18:32,840 --> 00:18:35,920
But again, it was skyscrapers, four-story buildings.

469
00:18:35,920 --> 00:18:38,200
Do you know what their different visions for beef are?

470
00:18:38,200 --> 00:18:40,040
Yeah, the one who was talking to me

471
00:18:40,040 --> 00:18:43,480
was the more pro-market beef vision.

472
00:18:43,480 --> 00:18:47,480
And the other one were the more regulation-oriented beef

473
00:18:47,480 --> 00:18:48,480
folks.

474
00:18:48,480 --> 00:18:50,720
Beef interests.

475
00:18:50,720 --> 00:18:52,360
So anyway, the idea of special interests

476
00:18:52,360 --> 00:18:55,360
is you've got some small group behind the scenes that

477
00:18:55,360 --> 00:18:59,000
undermines the will of the majority somehow.

478
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,240
And how is this possible?

479
00:19:01,240 --> 00:19:04,000
Again, the usual story goes back to rational ignorance again.

480
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,160
Special interests are not rationally ignorant

481
00:19:06,160 --> 00:19:09,520
because they have a lot riding on a particular issue,

482
00:19:09,520 --> 00:19:10,800
such as beef.

483
00:19:10,800 --> 00:19:14,040
And they have all kinds of detailed knowledge.

484
00:19:14,040 --> 00:19:16,240
And there's a reason to have that detailed knowledge

485
00:19:16,240 --> 00:19:20,640
because they are able to actually change beef policy.

486
00:19:20,640 --> 00:19:22,320
So regular voters are not informed.

487
00:19:22,320 --> 00:19:24,200
They have so little riding on the issue.

488
00:19:24,200 --> 00:19:25,700
What difference does it make to you

489
00:19:25,700 --> 00:19:27,700
whether there's some small change in beef policy

490
00:19:27,700 --> 00:19:29,200
personally?

491
00:19:29,200 --> 00:19:31,680
But on the other hand, people who spend their whole life

492
00:19:31,680 --> 00:19:33,120
in the beef industry, for them, it

493
00:19:33,120 --> 00:19:35,880
can be the difference between profits and bankruptcy.

494
00:19:35,880 --> 00:19:37,640
And there's even a slogan that goes along

495
00:19:37,640 --> 00:19:39,960
with this, which is concentrated benefits, diffuse costs.

496
00:19:39,960 --> 00:19:41,000
You may have heard of it.

497
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,680
So the benefits to the special interests are concentrated.

498
00:19:43,680 --> 00:19:46,600
The few industries that are closely involved,

499
00:19:46,600 --> 00:19:50,440
the fate of their business hangs in the balance.

500
00:19:50,440 --> 00:19:52,200
On the other hand, for a typical consumer,

501
00:19:52,200 --> 00:19:54,040
it's maybe a few bucks a year.

502
00:19:54,040 --> 00:19:55,960
So if you're a kid, did you ever sit around thinking,

503
00:19:55,960 --> 00:19:58,540
if I could just get every person on earth to give me a penny,

504
00:19:58,540 --> 00:20:01,040
I could get away and what I was getting paymentback on?


